                <td width="186" valign="top" class="row1" nowrap> <span class="largetext"><a name="3068"></a><b>joen00b</b></span><br />
                  <span class="smalltext">I AM WIN!<br />
                  <img src="images/avatars/gallery/401-500/481.gif" alt="" border="0" /><br />
                  <br />
                  Joined: 20 Nov 2002<br />
                  Posts: 536<br />
                  Location: Colorado Springs, Colorado</span><br>
                  <img src="templates/millenniumFalcon/images/spacer.gif" height="3"><br>
                  &nbsp;<a href="posting.php?mode=quote&amp;p=3068&amp;sid=8c8bdd26b886936a2e2fd0bff14ffe68"><img src="templates/millenniumFalcon/images/icon_quote_lang.gif" alt="Reply with quote" title="Reply with quote" border="0" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;
                  </td>
                <td valign="top" class="row2"> <span class="smalltext"><b></b></span>
                  <p><span class="largetext">From the fans perspective (and from myself who has been waiting patiently for 2 and a half freaking years) it's still Vapor Ware.  The fans are rabid for this, but they keep pushing back the release date.  I was one of the stupid people who feel for their Pre Order scam, and even to this day (some 5 months later) I still have the receipt on my cube wall to do nothing more than taunt myself.
<br />

<br />
Here are the facts as I see them:  The game does not have the cutting edge graphics as they were professing some years ago.  Why?  Well because it has been in development so long.  But when ya really think about it: Uber Graphics = Uber Lag when you're not running a top of the line Gaming PC.  To be honest, graphics aren't the end all be all as long as they <span style="font-style: italic">are</span> nice to look at.
<br />

<br />
Also, Wolfpack (the developers of Shadowbane) have a real chip on their shoulder towards Sony/Verant.  In fact, they have bagged on them endlessly in the past, but due to some legal wranglings have given up open derision of them and simply mutter under their breath now.  What makes this important about the release of Shadowbane?  It seems all their Beta Testing periods are now running neck and neck with SWG.  In fact, every time they hint at a release date, it's on par with SWG's release date.  SWG says January, a week later Wolfpack says their product will be ready in January.  SWG backs up release to March (tenatively) for more tweaking of code in beta, loe and behold SB now says March.  They want to release SB to go head to head with a Sony/Verant product.  They are slathering with this prospect.  I really believe they'll release the game the same month as the next Sony product (though knowing Wolfpack it will be EQ2 and not SWG) just to claim victory if the product does not sell it's projected units.  Even if SB fails miserably at first, as long as they can take a few bucks/customers from Sony, they'll claim victory.
<br />

<br />
Here is where it gets sticky though.  Ubisoft is the overseas distributor for EQ, well, SB went and signed them on for Domestic (by domestic I mean US because they are actually a British company) and Euro Distributor.  What this means, I have no clue, save for the fact that Ubisoft is making out like a bandit.  SB is just having problems finding a compny to host the server and bandwidth in Euro/Asia Markets, so it's kind of hard for them to come out and take the business from Sony, but rest assured, they are going to do everything they can to take copper from the coffers of Sony.
<br />

<br />
To give ou an analogy: It's Apple Vs IBM all over again.  SB is the scrappy young company ready to unseat the Corporate Giant.  This is where I talk about game play.
<br />

<br />
Why I think Shadowbane is going to be the best game and win shitloads of awards is because all they've put into it.  Sure, EQ has alot of history, but honestly, I remember us arguing over why Vox and Naggy were imprisoned on the boards and none of really knew why or of what import it had with Kerafrym.  Not so with Shadowbane.
<br />

<br />
They hired a professional writer to create a rich tapestry of history, full of strife and war and names and legends and myths.  Until the game is released, none of us will know for sure what is legend and what is myth, and then again, we may never know.  Also, this writer went above and beyond the history and wrote out a 5 year story line of escalating events that will shape the world that is COMPLETELY interactive with the player community.  They've already stated: Certain Races and Classes/Archetypes will be written out of the story.
<br />

<br />
Imagine this: When EQ first began, the only class that could be a Ranger was Wood Elf, then on Halloween there was a huge GM event in GFay and Kelethin gets sacked, the city is destroyed and gone forever.  Because of this the lore of the Rangers is lost.  Sure, you can still make a Wood Elf Character, but the Ranger option at the character selection screen is no longer there because the history of the world has changed and only a few of the Rangers are still alive and running around.  Impractical?  Sure!  Kick ass Roleplaying aspect?  Most F'ing definitely!  Then, imagine that the Dark Elves go to war and totally eradicate the race of High Elf.  At the character Selcetion screen, when making a new character, High Elf is no longer available because that race has been decimated and completely killed off.  Save for the few High Elf characters running around the world already, no more will ever be created.
<br />

<br />
Now the story goes: Once the world fell into Darkness, the denizens of the world will no longer die, and as such, no more people will be born.  It's like an undying hell on earth with no sun.  This is their excuse for bind points (instead of it just happening because you're paying them to store your characters on their servers) and a great example of how they have written all the tricks and twists that is MMORPG's into the game in terms of Roleplaying.  It's the little things like this that Wolfpack is doing for their customers, but there in lies the rub.
<br />

<br />
If you thought Verant were Nazis, you OBVIOUSLY haven't been to the Shadowbane messageboards.  These admins are straight dictators, and that is only a small portion of what you are going to taste once the game goes live.  The developers are not hiding the fact it's their game and you are wanting to play their creation.  They have very large egos, are ruthless and will not hesitate to kick your ass off their game.  They're going to bring player oppression to a whole new level with this game.
<br />

<br />
You'll have to weigh the pros and cons here and think about if it's worth it.  Here are some neat things about the game:  Above level 20, all hunting 'zones' where you would go for XP are PVP.  There are endless levels, and the game is geared towards leveling fast.  They want you level 40 in a month of starting just to give you a fighting chance in the PVP realms.  PVP will take up a huge portion of the game where you can siege player made cities (yes, as a guild, we could create our own city and when IoA or LoD gives us lip, we can go lay siege to their cities).  In these cities you can hire NPC guards (to protect your city), stonemasons (to build and repair the city) lumberjacks (to build and repair your city), merchants (of anything you can think of AND as the king of the realm you can set prices per faction or rival guilds), etc, etc, etc.  Also, in player made cities, the king collects tithes from guild members monthly.
<br />

<br />
Here is where it gets really neat:  If FD decided to take another guild under our wing, let's say LoD didn't have enough resources to build their own city, we would allow them use of our town and they would pay our guild a predsetermined amount of cash every month for use of our facilities.  If at any point they decide to not pay us, or not have the money to pay us, the king of the city would have a system set up on how to handle members in our town.  meaning, if they didn't pay, they could go from allied to KoS as soon as their payment wasn't met.  It also protects against subordination of said guild paying tithes.  If they decided to be inside the keep and attack our city attempting to sack it, they could find themselves trapped inside (as the city locks up) and all the NPCs whooping their asses.  I also believe there is an option for if another guild is paying tithes to the King of a city, they can't attack the NPC's or allied guild members.
<br />

<br />
And it's not that easy to siege a city.  You have to buy the lumberjacks (to vut down the trees and create siege weapons) engineers (to take out the walls) and have a big enough guild to fight man to man to protect the NPC's you hired to siege the town.  It also can take upwards of 24 RL Hours to successfully crush a large city.  That means the guild being logged in and fighting or your NPC's will abandon the siege.  People come and people go, but there must always be a large force helping out.
<br />

<br />
Another neat thing: You remember griefers?  Well, you can change you bind points at will in order to prevent people from bind point griefing you.  Also, in the early days of EQ when you consented someone, they would loot your corpse for you (not sure how many remember those days), well, in SB, when you die, unless you have binded items to your corpse, they get to take your shit.  Now, it's easy enough to get items bound to you, so it's not a big deal.  When you die, whatever is bound to you appears on you as you pop into your bind point.
<br />

<br />
The BEST part of the game?  Well, do you have friends on other servers in EQ?  In Shadowbane, you can use portals to go to other servers and live there happily forever and ever or go home to your own server.  In EQ terms, I would have a Wizard TL me to Tarew Marr server and while I was there I would be known as: Pizacatto Arpeggio of Cazic Thule.  Inversely, I would be Pizacatto Arpeggio of Tarew Marr if I came from there to CT.  I'm sure Lizzi and Bawd like the sound of that, eh?
<br />

<br />
Now, since I'm not in beta, I can only confirm about 70% of this from what I've gathered at all sorts of sites and such, and when you read it all again, you'll find it's nothing more than fluffy propoganda really.  There's not to much meat and potatoes about the game.
<br />

